# What a wrong model is permitted to take away, and what it is not.
#
# The plant model beside this file will be wrong. It was assembled from a
# service manual, a set of steam tables and a number of estimates from geometry,
# against a machine that has never been on a bench, and every value in it
# carries the fraction it may be out by. The useful question is not how wrong it
# is but what still holds when it is -- and that question has two answers, not
# one.
#
# Some behaviours must hold however wrong the model turns out to be. They are
# the ones whose failure is a machine that is unsafe, that damages itself, or
# that quietly does something other than what it was asked for, and none of them
# is allowed to depend on identification having succeeded -- because
# identification is the single thing most likely to be wrong on a first build.
# Others are permitted to get worse as the model does: they are how good the
# coffee is rather than whether the machine is fit to be switched on, and the
# design is entitled to trade them away to keep the first class intact.
#
# Without the split, a fit that lands badly threatens every property at once and
# nobody can say which ones still stand. With it, a bad fit has a known blast
# radius.
#
# This is data rather than a paragraph because its consumer is a later
# verification and not a reader. A paragraph cannot be checked for
# exhaustiveness, cannot be diffed when a behaviour is added, and cannot fail a
# build when a behaviour arrives carrying no class -- and an unclassified
# behaviour is the failure this exists to prevent, because it is the one that
# reads as covered to everybody who looks at it.
#
# Each line is `behaviour = class`. The admissible classes are declared in
# ../include/plant_robustness.h and nowhere else, so another one is added by a
# deliberate edit to that header rather than by a word being typed here and
# accepted. tools/check_robustness_declaration.py refuses a behaviour with no
# class, with two, with a word the header does not declare, a behaviour named
# twice, and a class with no behaviours in it at all.
#
# Nothing here names a control law, a structure, or a mechanism. These are
# statements about the machine's behaviour, and they have to outlive whatever
# loop is eventually built to deliver them. What each of them costs, and whether
# a particular loop actually holds one across the declared range, is the
# robustness verification's question -- there is no loop yet, and this file does
# not claim there is.

# --- What must hold however wrong the model is ------------------------------

# The machine is asked for things it cannot do: an extraction while the steam
# side is climbing, a temperature the supply cannot reach in the time allowed.
# Refusing is a decision about what is possible, and a model that is wrong about
# how fast a mass heats must not turn a refusal into an acceptance -- the caller
# has then been told the machine will do something it will not, and has no way
# to find out otherwise until it has not happened.
refusing-what-cannot-be-delivered = invariant

# Where a safe state is reached from, at what temperature, and how fast, are all
# quantities this model supplies badly. That it is reached at all cannot be one
# of them. A machine that fails to shut down because its model of the block was
# optimistic has failed in the one place where being wrong is not recoverable by
# a later correction.
reaching-a-safe-state = invariant

# The machine draws from a supply that cannot carry both elements and the pump
# at once, and the budget is a property of the wiring rather than of the model.
# A controller that overruns it because its model understated what an element
# draws has taken a decision the supply does not get to argue with; the trip is
# upstream and it opens on the truth, not on the estimate.
respecting-the-supply-budget = invariant

# Full duty is this machine's ordinary condition rather than an exception, so
# the actuator limit is where it spends much of its working life. What must hold
# there is that accumulated intent does not outlive the limit and arrive as an
# overshoot after the disturbance has passed. How much intent accumulates
# depends on the model; that it is surrendered when the actuator can take no
# more does not.
surrendering-accumulated-intent-at-the-actuator-limit = invariant

# --- What holds across the error this description declares ------------------

# Stability at nominal parameters demonstrates internal consistency and nothing
# about the machine. It is required across the whole declared range of model
# error, including its least convenient direction -- which is the one a fit will
# not warn about, because a fit reports how well it matched the data it was
# given and not how far the machine sits from it. It is not claimed beyond that
# range: a machine found to sit outside it has not been shown to be stable, and
# the answer is to establish it again against what was measured.
staying-stable-across-the-declared-error-range = bounded

# Where a margin stands between the machine and a hardware trip point, exhausting
# it is exactly the event the margin existed to prevent. The margin is therefore
# sized for what the model may be wrong by as well as for what the sensor may be
# wrong by -- the model error is the larger of the two -- and it must survive the
# model being wrong by the amount the description says it may be.
keeping-the-margin-that-protects-a-trip-point = bounded


# --- What is permitted to degrade as the model worsens ----------------------

# How closely a temperature is held is the quality of the coffee, and it
# degrades with the model honestly: a mass that is a third heavier than assumed
# is a slower loop, and a slower loop holds a setpoint less tightly. Declaring
# it here is not an excuse for it. It is a statement that its failure is a worse
# cup rather than an unsafe machine, and that the design may trade it away to
# keep everything above intact.
holding-a-setpoint-to-a-stated-tolerance = degrading

# The same reasoning for the transient rather than the steady state. How long
# the machine takes to become ready follows directly from coefficients nobody
# has measured -- a casting mass estimated from a drawing, an element rating the
# owner disputes -- and a design that promised a time independent of them would
# be promising something this description cannot support.
reaching-a-setpoint-within-a-stated-time = degrading
